// Exam archive: weeks 5-8 (original ÖSYM YDS-format items)

Object.assign(PASSAGES, {
  "w6-p1": "For more than a century, psychologists have known that information studied in several sessions spread over time is retained far better than the same information studied in a single, concentrated session. This phenomenon, known as the spacing effect, has been replicated with learners of all ages and with materials ranging from foreign vocabulary to surgical skills. Paradoxically, however, most students continue to rely on cramming, the practice of intensive study immediately before an exam. One explanation lies in the misleading sense of fluency that cramming produces: when material has just been reviewed, it feels familiar, and learners mistake this familiarity for durable knowledge. Spaced practice, by contrast, often feels more difficult, because some forgetting occurs between sessions and effort is needed to retrieve the material. Researchers argue that it is precisely this effort that strengthens long-term memory. Nevertheless, educational institutions have been slow to incorporate spacing into curricula, partly because its benefits become visible only weeks or months later, long after the course has ended and grades have been assigned.",
  "w6-p2": "The analysis of ancient DNA has transformed archaeology over the past two decades. Before these techniques became available, archaeologists inferred the movements of prehistoric peoples mainly from changes in material culture, such as pottery styles or burial customs. A new type of pot appearing in a region was often interpreted as evidence that newcomers had arrived and replaced the local population. Genetic studies have shown that this assumption was sometimes correct and sometimes badly mistaken. In certain cases, dramatic cultural shifts occurred with little or no change in the genetic makeup of a population, indicating that ideas and technologies had spread through trade or imitation rather than migration. In others, large-scale population turnover took place while many local traditions persisted. Some archaeologists, however, warn against allowing genetics to dominate the discipline. They point out that DNA can reveal who people's ancestors were, but not how those people understood their own identities, which were shaped by language, belief and social ties. For these scholars, genetic data are most valuable when combined with, rather than substituted for, traditional archaeological evidence.",
  "w6-p3": "It seems intuitive that improvements in energy efficiency should lead to a reduction in overall energy consumption. If cars travel farther on each litre of fuel, for instance, one might expect total fuel use to fall. Economists, however, have long drawn attention to a phenomenon known as the rebound effect. Because efficiency lowers the cost of using energy, consumers and firms may respond by using more of it: drivers of fuel-efficient cars might choose to drive longer distances, and savings on heating bills might be spent on energy-intensive goods. In its most extreme form, first described by the nineteenth-century economist William Stanley Jevons with reference to coal, the rebound may be so large that consumption actually rises. Recent studies suggest that such extreme cases are rare in modern economies; in most sectors, rebound effects offset only part of the expected savings. Even so, the phenomenon has important implications for climate policy. Analysts argue that efficiency measures are unlikely to achieve emissions targets on their own and should be accompanied by instruments, such as carbon taxes, that keep the price of energy from falling."
});

